According to Fajans' rule, covalent character increases with:

• {'paragraph': 'Small size of cation (higher polarizing power).'}
• {'paragraph': 'Large size of anion (higher polarizability).'}
• {'paragraph': 'Greater charge on cation.'}
• {'paragraph': 'Cations with pseudo noble gas configuration (e.g., \\(Cu^{+}\\), \\(Ag^{+}\\)) have higher polarizing power than noble gas configuration cations (e.g., \\(Na^{+}\\), \\(K^{+}\\)) of similar size and charge.'}

A. \(KF > KI; LiF > KF\)

• {'paragraph': 'For \\(KF\\) vs \\(KI\\): Cation \\(K^{+}\\) is common. Anion size: \\(F^{-} < I^{-}\\). Larger anion \\(I^{-}\\) means \\(KI\\) has more covalent character than \\(KF\\). So, \\(KF < KI\\). The statement \\(KF > KI\\) is **False**.'}
• {'paragraph': 'For \\(LiF\\) vs \\(KF\\): Anion \\(F^{-}\\) is common. Cation size: \\(Li^{+} < K^{+}\\). Smaller cation \\(Li^{+}\\) means \\(LiF\\) has more covalent character than \\(KF\\). So, \\(LiF > KF\\). This part is **True**.'}
• {'paragraph': 'Overall, statement A is **False**.'}

B. \(KF < KI; LiF > KF\)

• {'paragraph': 'Based on the analysis for A, both parts of this statement are **True**.'}
• {'paragraph': 'Overall, statement B is **True**.'}

C. \(SnCl_{4} > SnCl_{2}; CuCl > NaCl\)

• {'paragraph': 'For \\(SnCl_{4}\\) vs \\(SnCl_{2}\\): Cation charge: \\(Sn^{4+}\\) vs \\(Sn^{2+}\\). Higher charge on \\(Sn^{4+}\\) means \\(SnCl_{4}\\) has more covalent character than \\(SnCl_{2}\\). So, \\(SnCl_{4} > SnCl_{2}\\). This part is **True**.'}
• {'paragraph': 'For \\(CuCl\\) vs \\(NaCl\\): Anion \\(Cl^{-}\\) is common. Cations \\(Cu^{+}\\) (pseudo noble gas configuration) vs \\(Na^{+}\\) (noble gas configuration). \\(Cu^{+}\\) has higher polarizing power. So, \\(CuCl > NaCl\\). This part is **True**.'}
• {'paragraph': 'Overall, statement C is **True**.'}

D. \(LiF > KF; CuCl < NaCl\)

• {'paragraph': 'For \\(LiF > KF\\): This part is **True** (from A).'}
• {'paragraph': 'For \\(CuCl < NaCl\\): This part is **False** (from C).'}
• {'paragraph': 'Overall, statement D is **False**.'}

E. \(KF < KI; CuCl > NaCl\)

• {'paragraph': 'For \\(KF < KI\\): This part is **True** (from A).'}
• {'paragraph': 'For \\(CuCl > NaCl\\): This part is **True** (from C).'}
• {'paragraph': 'Overall, statement E is **True**.'}

Statements B, C, and E are correct.

Therefore, the correct option is (2) B, C, E only.
